\par
I created a horizontal architecture picture specifically for this RCA design, showing the interaction from the application through CloudWatch, Lambda, AgentCore Harness/Claude Sonnet, Gateway, and the KB/GitLab/Budibase targets.\par
\par
Download the horizontal RCA architecture diagram\par
\par
Architecture Interaction\par
Application and incident detection\par
Applications and infrastructure components continuously generate operational logs containing errors, exceptions, stack traces, warnings, and other diagnostic information.\par
Amazon CloudWatch Logs acts as the initial observation point. Log/subscription filters identify candidate events such as ERROR, FATAL, and exceptions without attempting to determine the actual root cause.\par
When a qualifying event is detected, it is forwarded to the RCA Trigger Lambda, starting an automated investigation.\par
RCA Trigger Lambda\par
The Lambda converts the raw CloudWatch event into structured incident context containing information such as application, environment, timestamp, error message, stack trace, log group/stream and correlation information.\par
It creates an RCA investigation session and invokes RcaHarness through AgentCore InvokeHarness.\par
The Lambda is therefore the event-driven entry point; it does not perform the detailed RCA itself.\par
AgentCore Harness and Claude Sonnet\par
RcaHarness manages the agent execution, including the system prompt, available tools, session context, model configuration, iteration limits and investigation flow.\par
Claude Sonnet is the reasoning model used by the Harness. It examines the incident, determines what additional evidence is needed and selects appropriate tools.\par
The investigation is iterative. For example, Sonnet may first retrieve historical incidents, then inspect a recent deployment, then examine the associated commit or merge request before reaching a conclusion.\par
AgentCore Gateway and tool selection\par
The Harness has rca-gateway configured as its tool-access layer. The Gateway exposes the available capabilities to the agent through MCP.\par
Sonnet selects a tool based on its name, description, input schema and the current investigation context. AgentCore then routes that request through the Gateway to the corresponding target.\par
This keeps reasoning separate from integration logic: Sonnet determines what information is required, while Gateway controls how that information is obtained.\par
Knowledge Base \f1\emdash  historical evidence\par
The Gateway's Knowledge Base target exposes a Retrieve tool. When historical evidence is needed, the request is routed to the Amazon Bedrock Knowledge Base.\par
Titan Embeddings V2 supports semantic/vector retrieval to identify historical chunks relevant to the current incident. The retrieved content is returned through Gateway and Harness to Claude Sonnet.\par
Sonnet then compares historical incidents, previous errors and resolutions with the current application failure. Titan performs retrieval; Sonnet performs the RCA reasoning.\par
GitLab \emdash  current engineering evidence\par
The GitLab Gateway target invokes the rca-gitlab-tools Lambda, which connects to the GitLab REST API.\par
Tools provide live information including projects, commits, code diffs, merge requests, approvals, pipelines, job logs, releases and deployments.\par
This allows Sonnet to answer questions such as: Was something deployed immediately before the incident? What code changed? Was the change approved? Did the associated pipeline contain failures?\par
Budibase \emdash  current incident-management evidence\par
The Budibase Gateway target similarly invokes a Lambda that communicates with the Budibase REST API.\f0\par
